artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $H = (H_{ij})_{1 \le i,j \le n}$ be a Hermitian random band matrix of size $n \times n$ with bandwidth $w = w(n) \in \{1,\dots,n\}$, defined as follows: the entries $H_{ij}$ with $|i-j| < w$ (interpreted on $\{1,\dots,n\}$, without periodic identification) are independent (up to the Hermitian symmetry $H_{ji} = \overline{H_{ij}}$), each centered with finite variance normalized so that $\sum_j \mathbb{E}|H_{ij}|^2 = 1$, while $H_{ij} = 0$ whenever $|i-j| \ge w$. Consider the local eigenvalue statistics of $H$ in the bulk of the spectrum, i.e. the joint distribution of rescaled eigenvalue gaps near a fixed energy $E$ in the interior of the limiting spectral support.

Prove or disprove the following dichotomy with sharp transition at bandwidth $w \sim \sqrt{n}$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $w \gg \sqrt{n}$ as $n \to \infty$, then the local eigenvalue statistics of $H$ in the bulk converge to those of the Gaussian Unitary Ensemble (GUE), i.e. are governed by the sine kernel determinantal process.
\item If $w \ll \sqrt{n}$ as $n \to \infty$, then the local eigenvalue statistics of $H$ in the bulk converge to a Poisson point process.
\end{enumerate}
